pf-perf	Platform	Performance	later	done	infra	First frame and large lists	The gallery and progress bundles' first frame on a slow connection, laziness in lists and lazy stacks (sw-lazy, sw-list-looks), a frame budget probe in CI over Examples/Landing. Done 2026-10-12 (Phase 9, item 44): List rows lazy like the lazy stacks', the bundles and first frames measured (Docs/elements/Performance.md: 4 MB gzip, about 21 s on 1.6 Mbps; the first frame 550-850 ms after the resources; scroll frames 7 ms median), scripts/frame-budget.sh and Playwright/frame-budget.mjs gating the landing page in CI (first frame 3 s, scroll median 40 ms); open: releasing far-away lazy elements, a smaller substrate. Found by Ember (2026-10-22): one layout of a sixty-comment thread (LazyVStack of rows that are an HStack of a VStack of HStacks of buttons with labels, each comment's HTML as AttributedString Texts) takes ~2.5 s in release and ~10 s in debug — 800 000 sizeThatFits calls, because nested stacks measure each child at the cross product of the widths and heights their parents probe (a Text sees 40 distinct proposals, 7 widths × 8 heights; Text's height proposal matters for its line trimming, so its memo cannot drop it). The thread settles after one layout; scrolling it relayouts. Open: fewer probes per level (a stack's min/max probes along its axis need not vary with every cross proposal), or a per-node memo keyed on what the node's measurement depends on. 2026-10-25 (Docs/elements/Performance.md, "A real app's frames"): the thread's cost was the lazy stack distributing its height (fixed: a lazy stack proposes nothing along its axis) and a stack proposing shares no memo matches (fixed: a one-size or bounded child takes its infinite answer); every scrolled frame had been a memo-dropping layout from a spinner's tick (repaint now), every update dropped every memo (an updated node's ancestry now), and blurs and shadows painted the whole canvas (their boxes now). Ember: feed scroll 80 → 18 ms a frame, thread 125 → 13–18, a lazy row 190–390 → 45, a full thread layout 1.5 s → 112 ms. Still open: ~10 µs a measurement (a release profile with names), updates that re-evaluate a whole screen. 2026-10-28: on the phone Ember was still seconds a frame — WebKit's canvas has no `filter`, so every blur ran the painter's per-pixel JavaScript Gaussian (σ 60 under a material bar at DPR 3: two thousand million multiplies a bar, two bars a frame; 430 ms a frame in Playwright's WebKit); the fallback draws the box down by ⌊σ/4⌋ and blurs by box passes (WebKit paint 420 → 7–11 ms, as Chrome).	2026-10-12	Playwright/landing-perf.mjs
